**Important Terms**

*Context bundle — Core unit delivered to models; contains payload and metadata.
*Bundle ID — Unique handle for fetch, cache, and references.
*Version — Enables reproducibility, rollback, and deterministic experiments.
*Provenance — Records origin/lineage for trust and audits.
*Signature — Cryptographic integrity/authenticity check for bundles.
*TTL / freshness — Controls staleness and cache eviction.
*Cache — Lowers latency and load while respecting TTL/policies.
*Fetcher / registry API — Source-of-truth retrieval and authorization surface.
*Schema / bundle spec — Ensures parseability, validation, and compatibility.
*Policy metadata — Encodes privacy, compliance, and RBAC rules attached to bundles.
*Redaction / transformation hooks — Apply policy-driven masking or alteration before delivery.
*Retrieval hints — Guide relevance (embeddings, selectors, prompt cues) to reduce noise.
*Merge / resolution strategy — Defines precedence when combining multiple contexts.
*Deterministic routing — Maps bundles to model versions for consistent behavior.
*Audit trail / event log — Records fetches, attachments, and policy decisions for compliance.
*Confidence / provenance scores — Per-item trust metrics used for filtering/weighting.
*Session state / correlation id — Ties context to user sessions for continuity and tracing.
*Content-addressable id / checksum — Immutable id and integrity guarantee for deduplication.
*Enforcement hook / policy engine — Runtime evaluator that accepts/rejects/modifies bundles.
*Auditability / reproducibility — Outcome of combining versioning, signatures, logs, and routing.

All are part of a practical MCP design; some (e.g., signature, provenance, schema, policy hooks) are high-priority for
safety and auditability, while others (e.g., merge strategy, retrieval hints) are implementation/optimization concerns.
